We have conducted an in-depth analysis of the vector-like T quark pair production at future high-energy electron-positron colliders, focusing on both $ee ightarrow T \overline{T}$ and $ee ightarrow bW^{+}T \overline{T}$ processes. Our study employed the $ee ightarrow T \overline{T} ightarrow b \ell^{+} 
u_{\ell} b \overline{b} \ell^{-} 
u_{\ell}$ final state, where $\ell$ represents either an electron or a muon. Our analysis utilized the $ee ightarrow T \overline{T}$ process to determine the vector-like T quark mass and the $W^{\pm}Z$ coupling of the T quark, while the $ee ightarrow b \ell^{+} 
u_{\ell} b \overline{b} \overline{b} 
u_{\ell}$ final state was used to investigate the $W^{\pm}B$ coupling. Our findings indicate that a future high-energy electron-positron collider can precisely measure the T quark mass and the $W^{\pm}Z$ coupling with uncertainties of $0.1\%$ and $1\%$, respectively, and the $W^{\pm}B$ coupling can be measured with an uncertainty of $1.5\%$.
